    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Rock mechanical considerations affect many aspects of hydrocarbon exploration and production,\r\nranging from drilling, production-induced subsidence via reservoir permeability and compartmentalization to hydrocarbon migration and trapping. In this contribution we discuss how the computer program Poly3d can be used to obtain first order insights into some of these problems. The approach taken here are based on the concept that the seismically mapped fault pattern, the fault slip distribution, and structure contour maps contain information about the deformation and loading history of the reservoir rock. A 3D model of the faulted reservoir and its surroundings can be build. Such a model, combined with an appropriate set of boundary conditions, can then be used in Poly3d to compare the mapped slip distribution to theoretical fault slip distribution; to compute the stress perturbation in the reservoir; and to investigate various regional geological deformation scenarios. Some subsurface examples are used to illustrate how Poly3d can be used to increase confidence in a seismic interpretation, to address jointing in the interwell space, andto investigate the effects of alternative loadinghypotheses",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Willemse, Emanuel J.M., Karpuz, Ridvan M., Maerten, Laurent, Frøyland, Lars A. and Fristad, Trobjørn. (1996). Application of Poly3D to Subsurface Problem. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/rm881mq2084",
